---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-klaviyo';
const title = 'Yotpo Email & SMS to Klaviyo for Shopify: Migration Assessment';
const desc = 'Profile migration without mistaking imported profiles for consent or engagement after Yotpo messaging retirement.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I keep using Yotpo Email & SMS?",
    "answer": "No. The messaging product is retired; assess a replacement sender and keep Reviews and Loyalty as separate decisions."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should decide whether Klaviyo fits?",
    "answer": "Profile migration without mistaking imported profiles for consent or engagement. Use the proposed acceptance cases in this guide before approving production enrollment."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does importing contacts restore every journey?",
    "answer": "No. Permissions, message history, dynamic segments and waiting automation contacts need separate reconciliation. Import success is not a sending approval."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Include Sequenzy if it is on your shortlist, using the same consent, Shopify event and operating-cost requirements. This page has not verified its current plan terms or migration tooling; it does not establish a price or feature advantage."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Yotpo to Klaviyo</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="catalog-kicker">Migration assessment</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS to Klaviyo for Shopify</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Profile migration without mistaking imported profiles for consent or engagement. This guide proposes checks for a replacement program; it does not report merchant tests or measured outcomes.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>A retired sender changes the decision</h2>
      <p>Yotpo announced messaging retirement for the end of 2025. Its application now displays a decommission notice. Compare destinations and recovery options, rather than choosing to remain on that sender. <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo lifecycle notice</a>.</p>
      <h2>Read the current migration scope</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo documents a one-time, one-way Yotpo profile sync. The current guide says it does not import email/SMS engagement or consent; consent CSVs and email suppressions are separate steps. This matters when older marketplace descriptions imply broader transfer coverage. Use the current help procedure and obtain account-specific confirmation, especially because the retired Yotpo dashboard may no longer supply credentials or exports.</p>
      <h2>Separate audience membership from permission</h2>
      <p>Prepare a mapping ledger with email permission, SMS permission, suppression reason and supporting timestamp/source where available. Keep each channel independent. An imported phone number or Yotpo tag is not proof of SMS permission. Reconcile opted-out records before activating sends; where source records are missing, record that gap rather than manufacturing a subscription date.</p>
      <h2>Do not invent engagement recency</h2>
      <p>A proposed reactivation segment must not treat a newly imported profile as newly engaged. Preserve any usable historical engagement archive separately, including how it was measured. If that archive is missing, design the first audience from independently available evidence and permission, and document its limitations. Do not label the entire imported list active merely because the import succeeded.</p>
      <h2>Proposed enrollment test</h2>
      <p>Before importing a controlled sample, disable list-triggered flows that should not enroll existing customers. Include an opted-out customer, an email-only subscriber, a dual-channel subscriber and a previous purchaser. Inspect their destination statuses and flow membership. Only the intended eligible cases may enter rebuilt journeys. Repeat the checks after a fresh opt-out, so a later file cannot silently restore an older subscribed state.</p>
      <h2>Rebuild the retained Yotpo connections</h2>
      <p>Reviews and Loyalty need their own connector and event inventory. Specify which system collects reviews, which sender requests them, and how a submitted review cancels a pending request. For Loyalty, verify the fields used for tier or points messages instead of substituting a stale imported tag. Klaviyo is a candidate when these explicit data boundaries fit your retention program; it is not an automatic winner for every store with Yotpo Reviews.</p>
      <h2>History and active queues need different treatment</h2>
      <p>Keep Shopify order history, Yotpo message history and consent evidence as distinct records. For every still-active destination queue, record its sender, next action and stop condition. Waiting contacts are operational state, not simply an audience export. Reconstruct only the messages still justified by current purchase and permission data. If an import or event mapping fails, pause the replacement journey and reconcile it; the decommissioned service is not a rollback destination.</p>
      <h2>Price the work you will retain</h2>
      <p>Request current plan terms for the eligible audience, expected sends, channel usage and required integrations. Add retained Reviews/Loyalty charges and one-time reconstruction labor separately. Compare a normal month with your own peak calendar. No supplier price, revenue uplift or payback period is assumed here. A lower messaging quote can still leave more manual work if historical data or queue state cannot be recovered.</p>
      <h2>Evidence and remaining limits</h2>
      <p>Checked November 24, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/39598076123547">Klaviyo implementation documentation</a>. The acceptance designs above are editorial proposals. Account access, recovery availability, actual event payloads, legal sufficiency of permission records and live delivery remain untested. Obtain the missing evidence before enabling the affected journeys.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Migration guide</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Pricing models</a></p>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Klaviyo vs Yotpo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-omnisend">Yotpo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo">Sequenzy vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">Reviews & UGC playbook</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/jewelry">Email tools for jewelry stores</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Yotpo to Klaviyo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
